  "id": "ch6-tactics-and-plans",
  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "intro": "Five positions from the lines you have just learnt, each stopping on the move that matters. Play through the moves and find the last one.\n\n## What these are, and what they are not\n\nTwo of the five end on a genuine tactic: a knight that wins a pawn because the piece defending it has been removed, and a knight move that defends a pawn by attacking the square the attacker would have to stand on. The other three end on a decision, which is the more common kind of moment in a real game. Knowing that ...c4 is better than the queen trade will win you more games than any single combination in this opening, and it is not a tactic.\n\n## The themes worth carrying away\n\nRemove the defender. In the Colle, the pawn on d4 is held up by the knight on f3. Trade the knight off first and the pawn falls; hit the pawn first and nothing happens.\n\nDefend by attacking. In the Exchange Slav, ...Na5 guards b7 because it attacks b7, so the queen that was going to take there loses herself instead.\n\nWatch the diagonal behind your own piece. In the 6.Ne5 line Black's knight on e5 is pinned against the queen on c7, and the pin is invisible until you move the knight and lose the queen.\n\nSpace before trades. Against the London, pushing the pawn to c4 keeps a queenside bind that a queen trade on b3 hands straight back.\n\nStrike the wedge. When White's pawn reaches e5 in the main line, ...f6 is the move that stops the position going quietly downhill.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying the capture that looks like the tactic before the preparation that makes it work. Trading queens because the trade is offered. Moving a piece that is holding a diagonal shut. And reaching a comfortable position and then not having a plan, which is the most expensive mistake on this list.",
